\pard\sa240\b\fs28 20. Duplicate-folder cleanup utility - tlo-deleteDupes\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160 tlo-deleteDupes searches one fully qualified Input Path recursively for duplicate sibling folders. Candidate discovery combines exact copy families and same-artist/date matching. An exact copy family includes folders such as X, X (copy2), and X (copy3), so numbered copies are compared with one another even when X is absent or has different contents. TLO also compares same-parent folders when their normalized artist and date match even if the rest of the folder names differ.\par
\pard\sa160 Candidate folders are divided into content-equivalence clusters after TLO compares their complete recursive manifests. Copies are compared with one another as well as with unsuffixed folders. Each cluster of two or more identical trees keeps one folder and moves the other identical members. TLO prefers to keep a folder without a terminal (copyN) suffix. If more than one unsuffixed folder in the cluster is identical, the alphabetically first name is kept. If a cluster contains only numbered copies, the lowest-numbered copy is kept. Different content clusters remain as separate variants. For example, if X differs from X (copy2) and X (copy3), but copy2 and copy3 match one another, TLO keeps X and copy2 and moves copy3.\par
\pard\sa160 Same-artist/date discovery recognizes a standalone four-digit 19xx/20xx year, yyyy-mm-dd style dates (including xx month/day components), and four-digit-year ranges. An unknown date without an actual four-digit year is not enough to broaden duplicate discovery. A matching artist and date is only a way to select folders for comparison; it does not by itself make a folder a duplicate.\par
\pard\sa160 Before any candidate folder is moved, TLO requires the same relative nested folder structure, including empty folders, and the same relative file names and file sizes as another member of its content-equivalence cluster. This cleanup utility deliberately does not compare file bytes or timestamps when deciding whether folders are duplicates. A folder whose manifest differs remains in its own content cluster rather than being discarded merely because another folder was previously chosen as a master.\par
\pard\sa160 When two folders in the same candidate component do not match, TLO appends one short CSV line to TLOHome/deleteDupesMismatches.txt. Copies are compared with one another as well as with unsuffixed folders. The three fields are folder name, folder name, simple reason. Examples include different number of files, different sub-structure, different file names, and 01.flac different sizes. Only folder names are recorded; full paths are not included. Folder names containing commas are automatically CSV-quoted. deletedDirs.txt remains only the list of folders actually moved.\par
\pard\sa160 Before any duplicate member of a content-equivalence cluster is moved into the duplicates holding folder, TLO checks every FLAC file in that cluster's kept folder tree by fully decoding its audio stream with the bundled ffmpeg validator. This is a full audio-integrity check, not merely a FLAC header or tag read.\par
\pard\sa160 Each full FLAC decode has a 180-second timeout. A timeout means the keeper is unverifiable, not healthy or corrupt. If any kept-folder FLAC times out, TLO reports the condition and leaves that entire duplicate cluster in place rather than relocating its other members.\par
\pard\sa160 If a FLAC in the kept folder is corrupt, TLO looks for the same relative file in the other members of that same content-equivalence cluster. Copy-suffixed repair sources are tried in numeric order: copy1, copy2, copy3, and so on. Qualifying unsuffixed repair sources are tried alphabetically. The first counterpart that fully decodes without errors is copied into the kept folder, installed in place of the corrupt file, and checked again after replacement. Nested FLAC files are handled by their relative paths.\par
\pard\sa160 If no healthy replacement is available, TLO reports the corrupt FLAC and continues. An unrepaired corrupt FLAC does not cause otherwise matching duplicates to be retained. The selected keeper for that content-equivalence cluster is kept, and every other identical cluster member is still moved as a complete folder tree into the duplicates holding folder.\par
\pard\sa160 TLO places qualifying duplicates in a folder named duplicates at the root of the partition containing the Input Path. TLO creates that folder when it does not already exist. On Windows, E:\\Music therefore uses E:\\duplicates. On Linux/macOS, a search under /mnt/music uses /mnt/music/duplicates when /mnt/music is the filesystem mount root. TLO moves the entire qualifying duplicate folder tree in one same-partition directory move; it does not move child files individually. If an entry with the same name already exists in duplicates, TLO preserves both by adding a collision suffix such as (moved 2). If the search includes the partition root, TLO excludes the duplicates holding folder from further duplicate discovery. After a successful move, TLO appends the folder's full original pre-move path to TLOHome/deletedDirs.txt.\par
\pard\sa160 TLOHome is resolved using the same --TLOHome and TLOHome environment-variable logic used by the other TLO mains. The compatibility alias remains accepted but is intentionally omitted from user-facing help and documentation. The Input Path itself must be a fully qualified existing directory.\par
\pard\sa160 Example: tlo-deleteDupes.cmd --TLOHome C:\\TLOHome E:\\Music\par
\pard\sa160 On Linux or macOS source execution: python3 tlo-deleteDupes.py --TLOHome /path/to/TLOHome /path/to/music\par
\pard\sa160 Ctrl-C stops further work. The program closes deletedDirs.txt and deleteDupesMismatches.txt while unwinding and exits with cancellation status 130. Entries successfully logged before the interrupt remain in their respective log files; any folder already moved into the partition-root duplicates holding folder remains there.\par

\par \page
\pard\sa240\b\fs28 21. Research log lookup - tlo-research\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160 Research provides a focused way to inspect the metadata and complete-path evidence already recorded in TLOHome/logs. The same search engine is used by the standalone tlo-research console application and by the Research button on the main Inventory GUI.\par
\pard\sa160 The input string is one of three forms: an artist followed by a date, a venue, or a date. Examples are Grateful Dead 1977-05-08, Barton Hall, 1977-05-08, and xxxx-xx-xx. Research uses the same canonical date parser as Inventory, so every Inventory-supported date representation is accepted, including numeric dates, textual-month dates, compact dates, slash dates, supported year ranges, partial x-placeholder forms such as 2001-04-1x, 2004-0x-xx, 202x-xx-xx, 19xx-xx-xx, 20xx-xx-xx, and xxxx-xx-xx, plus a standalone four-digit 19xx/20xx year. Research normalizes the date before matching. A date-only search matches the DATE field; artist plus date requires both ARTIST and DATE. Any other input is treated as a venue and is matched case-insensitively against VENUE.\par
\pard\sa160 Research reads every meta*.log in TLOHome/logs and returns the complete SHOW_NAME through END_SHOW_METADATA block for every matching record. It then uses MAIN_DIR_PATH and MUSIC_DIRS_JSON to locate every corresponding path line in comp*.log and displays those under COMP LOG. Research does not stop after the first metadata match. After the structured results, it performs a second exhaustive raw-line scan of every meta*.log and comp*.log and displays an ALL RELATED RAW LOG LINES section with every directly related occurrence, its log filename, and its line number. There is no per-file or per-query hit limit. This raw evidence is displayed even when there are zero structured metadata matches, which makes it possible to trace historical source strings or intermediate evidence that did not survive into the final metadata record.\par
\pard\sa160 In the Inventory GUI, click Research and type the research string in the input box. Search remains the active/default action whenever the Research dialog is active, so Return or keypad Enter anywhere in that dialog runs the search. Results open in a read-only, non-wrapping text window with vertical scrolling and a horizontal scrollbar along the bottom. Ctrl-A selects the complete result even when a button owns focus. The results window has a Search button, and Ctrl-F invokes the same action. The find dialog has a Search for: box and mutually exclusive Forward and Backwards choices, with Forward selected by default. Repeated searches continue in the chosen direction, ignore case, and wrap to the opposite end when there is no later/earlier match. The GUI uses the TLOHome already resolved when the Inventory app started.\par
\pard\sa160 In the CLI, output is written to the console. TLOHome can be supplied with --TLOHome; otherwise the TLOHome environment variable is used. The standalone app follows the same hidden compatibility precedence as the other TLO mains.\par
\pard\sa160 Windows example: tlo-research.exe --TLOHome C:\\TLOHome "Grateful Dead 1977-05-08"\par
\pard\sa160 Windows venue example: tlo-research.exe --TLOHome C:\\TLOHome "Barton Hall"\par
\pard\sa160 Linux/macOS example: tlo-research --TLOHome /path/to/TLOHome 1977-05-08\par
\pard\sa160 If no matching metadata records are found, Research prints a zero-match result rather than treating that as an error. Missing TLOHome/logs, invalid TLOHome, or empty input are reported as errors. Ctrl-C stops the standalone app with cancellation status 130.\par
\par\b Build 385 setlist.fm upgraded access\b0\par
Build 385 adds a setlist.fm upgrade checkbox directly beneath setlist.fm. When both are selected, TLO changes request spacing from the normal 600 milliseconds to 1/14 second (14 requests per second) and replaces the normal 1,400-per-run cap with a shared 48,000-request UTC-day quota. The upgraded daily quota is coordinated across TLO runs through TLOHome/logs/setlistfm-state. Selecting setlist.fm upgrade without setlist.fm has no effect.\par
\par\b Build 384 Reverse log identification and changed partition identity\b0\par
Build 384 broadens the reverse input to Original Partition / Path, allowing a current volume label, drive/root, or full path such as D:\\somePath. The historical volume label and drive letter may both have changed. TLO identifies one applicable combined Copy/Delete + Rename success log using path evidence, exact destination matches, and tolerant artist+date evidence that recognizes Last, First / First Last and Name, The / The Name forms. After one log is selected, the reverse worker does not search other logs again and does not use fuzzy matching to choose folders. Each restore still requires the exact logged destination path; manually renamed or moved folders are left untouched and reported as skipped unmatched. The selected log is shown before confirmation and recorded in runSettings.log and reverseCopyDelete.log.\par
\par\b Build 383 Reverse Copy/Delete + Rename\b0\par
Build 383 adds a main-window Reverse Copy/Delete + Rename action for the combined Full Inventory Tag Copy/Delete Original plus Rename Compliantly workflow. The reverse window asks for the original partition name and the Copy/Delete destination. A visible volume/partition label is preferred and is resolved to its currently mounted root, allowing a Windows drive-letter change since the forward run; a rooted drive/path is accepted as a fallback. The function uses only exact successful TAG_COPY_DELETE source-to-destination mappings from logs that confirm copy-and-delete plus compliant rename, and restores the exact original folder names and locations without changing tags. Existing original paths are protected from overwrite. Same-filesystem restores move directories; cross-filesystem restores copy and verify the complete tree before removing the moved copy. Results are appended to reverseCopyDelete.log and the action is recorded in runSettings.log.\par

\par\b Build 382 structured setlist artist correction\b0\par
Build 382 treats the Artist line of a strict unlabeled selected-setlist header as artist evidence as well as a structural header line. The unlabeled artist must resolve uniquely in artists.sqlite before TLO can use it. A DB-confirmed structured setlist artist may correct an incompatible artist supported only by weaker generic path or subdirectory scanning and may resolve a generic path-only artist conflict. It does not override $slam, tag-backed artists, explicit folder-pattern or commercial-release artist rules, setlist-filename artist evidence, Artist DB collisions, or other stronger/non-path artist sources. The reported Kinky Friedman / Lone Star Cafe, New York, NY header therefore resolves to Kinky Friedman xxxx-xx-xx Lone Star Cafe New York, NY even if an erroneous Lone Star Cafe artist entry still exists in artists.sqlite.\par
\par\b Build 381 setlist location parsing correction\b0\par
Build 381 prevents a state name embedded inside an artist or title line from being mistaken for a trailing City + State location. The trailing-region fallback now requires the state/region to end the location expression, so The Georgia Satellites cannot become The, GA. A narrow comma-delimited location rule also accepts safe mixed-case state codes at the end of a location line; Santa Cruz,Ca resolves to Santa Cruz CA. Ambiguous/common-word mixed-case state abbreviations remain protected. The reported setlist header now resolves to Venue The Catalyst and Location Santa Cruz CA.\par
\par\b Build 380 compliant dash-date precedence\b0\par
Build 380 adds a narrow compliant String1 - String2 Date check after String1 Date String2 and before the generic String1 - String2 fallback. If String2 begins or ends with one complete performance date, TLO normalizes that Date and preserves the remaining String2 text as raw album/show/venue text. The reported The Georgia Satellites - Santa Cruz 01-17-87 case therefore becomes The Georgia Satellites 1987-01-17 Santa Cruz. Bare years, ranges, partial dates, and middle-of-String2 dates are not reclassified by this new branch. Add Shows uses the same pattern for duplicate/new-show handling.\par
\par\b Build 379 track-title technical-table filtering\b0\par
Build 379 prevents Exact Audio Copy (EAC) extraction logs and TOC tables from being selected as numbered song lists. Numeric multi-column rows containing timestamps/sectors are rejected as technical data even when they appear as a perfect 1..N sequence. EAC markers start a technical tail that is not mined for titles. An explicit local Songs: or Tracks: block is retained as real local song-list evidence even when its title count differs from the number of audio files; the mismatch is reported, but TLO does not invent a one-to-one mapping.\par
\par\b Build 378 artist abbreviation precedence\b0\par
Build 378 makes short no-space-date artist abbreviations weak evidence in non-compliant inventory. A prefix such as gd in gd1977-05-08... is deferred while TLO checks stronger artist evidence from usable tags, full DB-backed path names, selected-setlist metadata and filename evidence, and other structured fallbacks. The abbreviation-bearing path component is excluded from the ordinary path artist scan so the same short prefix cannot be promoted early a second way. If no stronger artist is identified, the abbreviation may be used only as the final local fallback through artists.sqlite. When an exact date and an enabled eTreeDB or setlist.fm lookup are available, TLO attempts the artist/date lookup after selecting the abbreviation and logs a successful lookup as additional confirmation; otherwise the log records that the last-resort abbreviation remained unconfirmed. Music filenames remain usable for date fallback, but their short artist-style prefixes do not outrank stronger artist evidence.\par
\par\b Build 377 Research window usability\b0\par
Build 377 adds a horizontal scrollbar along the bottom of the Research results box and makes Search the active/default Research dialog action. Pressing Enter while the Research input dialog has focus runs Search even when the text-entry control itself does not own focus.\par
\par\b Build 376 century-known partial dates\b0\par
Build 376 accepts 19xx-xx-xx and 20xx-xx-xx as legitimate partial dates throughout the canonical Inventory date parser. In Compliant mode, a folder such as Duke Ellington 19xx-xx-xx Danish Radio is parsed as Artist Duke Ellington, Date 19xx-xx-xx, and Album/Show text Danish Radio rather than falling back to unknown Artist/Album tags. The same canonical rule is inherited by Research. Broader 1xxx/2xxx years and century-known years followed by known month/day components remain invalid.\par
\par\b Build 375 venue/location correction\b0\par
Build 375 prevents festival/archive numbering from becoming venue/location text. A path tail such as 04 05.Reidsville NC is interpreted as Reidsville, NC with no numeric venue; it is not stored as Venue 04 / City 05.Reidsville. When selected-setlist metadata later supplies a higher-confidence venue/location, it may replace conflicting values that were supported only by weaker path-part evidence. Strong eTreeDB or other non-path evidence is preserved. For example, Camp Springs Bluegrass Festival | Reidsville, NC replaces a weak path-derived 04 | 05.Reidsville, NC result.\par

\par\b Build 374 Research completeness and date classification\b0\par
Research uses the canonical Inventory date parser, so forms such as xxxx-xx-xx, 19xx-xx-xx, 20xx-xx-xx, 202x-xx-xx, textual dates, compact dates, slash dates, and supported ranges are recognized as date or artist-plus-date queries and normalized before metadata matching. Standalone four-digit 19xx/20xx years remain accepted Research dates. Build 374 also adds the exhaustive raw meta/comp log pass described above so Research continues through every related occurrence instead of ending with the structured metadata/correlated-comp result.\par
\par\b Build 371 artist identification corrections\b0\par
In non-compliant inventory, a tag ARTIST or ALBUMARTIST value that resolves unambiguously in the Artist DB remains strong artist evidence. If the candidate tag artist does not resolve in the Artist DB, TLO now defers that raw tag instead of allowing it to block stronger path or selected-setlist artist evidence. The raw tag remains available only as a last-resort fallback when no stronger artist can be identified.\par
Metadata path parsing also distinguishes a folder that is itself a wrapper from a meaningful show folder that merely contains wrapper terminology. For example, Firefall 1976-05-xx Cleveland FM Broadcast remains eligible for Artist/Date parsing, while a standalone FM Broadcast folder remains a wrapper.\par
This prevents track-title or date-like tags such as 01 - Introduction - WMMS or 1976-05 from overriding an obvious DB-backed path artist such as Firefall.\par
\par\b Build 373 additional artist identification safeguards\b0\par
In non-compliant inventory, unmatched numeric-only ARTIST/ALBUMARTIST values such as 01 or 02 and unmatched date-like values such as 1976-05 are treated as artist-tag noise rather than deferred artist fallbacks. TLO continues checking later sampled ARTIST values and then ALBUMARTIST values. A value that looks numeric but resolves in the Artist DB remains valid, so legitimate DB-backed numeric artist names are preserved.\par
DB-backed path artist lookup now tries exact folder components first, then a simple Last, First reversal such as Ellington, Duke to Duke Ellington, and then the longest Artist-DB-backed phrase embedded in a descriptive folder name. This lets a folder such as Duke Ellington, Unissued recordings by Danish Radio 1957-70 identify Duke Ellington without accepting arbitrary unmatched substrings.\par
If a potential artist name ends with Band or Group, a common All Star/All Stars spelling, or terminal All Star Band / All-Star Band, and the complete name is not found in the Artist DB, TLO retries once without that terminal suffix. Supported All-Star forms remain All Star, All Stars, All-Star, All-Stars, AllStar, and AllStars; the Band-combination forms also accept All Stars Band and All-Stars Band. Matching is case-insensitive. The full name is always tried first. The shorter retry is used only when it resolves uniquely. When it does, TLO uses the Artist DB master to identify the base artist, restores the exact terminal text that was stripped, and uses the expanded restored name as the artist for that performance. If the DB master already ends in a supported grouping suffix, TLO does not append a second suffix. Ambiguous shorter matches are not guessed, and nonterminal suffix text is not stripped. Restored expanded performance names that are not full DB masters are added to TLOHome/artistsNotInDatabase.txt for later Artist DB review.\par
If path and explicit setlist-content artist evidence still do not identify an artist, TLO may use the selected setlist filename as an artist-only DB-backed fallback. For example, Duke ELLINGTON, Unissued Ellington by Danish Radio, 1957-1970, FM.txt can identify Duke Ellington. The filename is not used to infer date, venue, or location, and this fallback still outranks an unmatched raw audio ARTIST tag. A partial one-word database artist inside a longer filename is accepted only with stronger filename structure: the artist must be the entire filename stem, or a leading component whose first substantive component after the artist is a recognizable date, allowing only normal filename separators before that date; an immediate explicit delimiter may also establish structure. For example, Two Gentlemen.txt and Two Gentlemen 1979-10-05.txt do not identify artist Two merely because Two exists in the Artist DB, while Prince 1984-06-07.txt can still identify Prince.\par

\par\b Build 386 - Main inventory console horizontal scrolling\b0\par
- Replaced the main Inventory console wrapping ScrolledText with a non-wrapping Text widget.\par
- Added independent vertical scrolling and a bottom horizontal scrollbar so long paths/log lines remain on one line.\par
\par\b Build 387 - String1 - String2 performance evidence before album fallback\b0\par
- Ordinary non-compliant String1 - String2 now inspects String2 for a complete performance date before the generic Artist - Album fallback.\par
- After removing a recognized date, conservative venue/location parsing may establish performance metadata only when city plus state/region or country is strongly anchored and unexplained trailing text is absent.\par
- Explicit leading-year Artist - Album commercial-release forms retain their existing behavior; ambiguous album titles still fall back to album mode.\par
\par\b Build 388 - Compact setlist Artist/Date header lines\b0\par
- Selected setlist headers may identify Artist Date or Artist Date Venue on one line.\par
- The date token anchors the split; short header-like text before it is artist evidence and a plausible/known venue after it may be retained.\par
- Existing prose, track-list boundary, location, and Artist DB confirmation safeguards remain in effect.\par
\par\b Build 389 - FLAC ALBUMARTIST precedence\b0\par
- Within low-priority FLAC tag evidence, ALBUMARTIST is evaluated before ARTIST.\par
- Tag evidence remains below stronger path, structured-setlist, selected-setlist-filename, $slam, and other established higher-priority artist evidence.\par
\par\b Build 390 - Nested opening-act discovery\b0\par
- Phase 1 and Phase 2/3 now continue descending into child directories after a parent music folder is recorded.\par
- A nested music folder can therefore be discovered as an independent opening act.\par
- Existing wrapper/grouping rules remain responsible for keeping ordinary CD/Disc/Set/volume structures together.\par
\par\b Build 391 - Acceptable corruption percentage\b0\par
- Added an "acceptable corruption %" main-window text box and --acceptable-corruption-percent CLI option.\par
- Input is limited to integers 0 through 100 inclusive; default is 100.\par
- Before rename/copy/delete/tag mutations, TLO checks directly inventoried logical-show audio files and removes only when corrupt_files * 100 > acceptable_percent * total_audio_files.\par
- When the threshold is exceeded, TLO sends the logical show folder to the operating-system Trash/Recycle Bin and omits it from inventory only after that move succeeds.\par
- If Trash/Recycle Bin fails, TLO never permanently deletes as a fallback; it logs CORRUPTION_REMOVAL_FAILED and keeps the folder/inventory record.\par
- Full Inventory Dry Run reports WOULD_REMOVE_CORRUPTION without changing files.\par

\par\b Build 392 - Terminal Band Artist DB fallback\b0\par
- If a potential artist candidate ends in Band and the complete name is not found in the Artist DB, TLO retries after removing the final word Band.\par
- A complete ... Band database match always wins; the shorter form is only a secondary no-match fallback.\par
- Only one unique shorter-form database result is accepted, and the Artist DB master name is used.\par
\par\b Build 393 - Acceptable corruption default\b0\par
- Changed the default acceptable corruption percentage from 0 to 100.\par
- The valid range remains integers 0 through 100 inclusive, and explicitly entering 0 still selects a zero-percent threshold.\par
- Threshold math, Trash/Recycle Bin safety, Dry Run behavior, and removal logging are unchanged.\par\par\b Build 394 - Linux CI corruption-fixture hardening\b0\par
- Five legacy regression tests that intentionally use invalid FLAC placeholder bytes now explicitly set acceptable corruption % to 100 because those tests exercise copy/delete, rename, tagging, and unidentified-show behavior rather than corruption removal.\par
- This makes the tests deterministic on generic Linux runners where gio trash is available and can successfully remove the temporary fixture folder.\par
- Runtime application behavior is unchanged from Build 393.\par
\par\b Build 395 - Setlist double-dash venue/location and short-venue protection\b0\par
- Recognizes concise Location -- Venue and Venue -- Location selected-setlist headers before generic venue-list fallback.\par
- The reported Winnipeg, MB -- CBC Winnipeg studios header resolves to Venue CBC Winnipeg studios and Location Winnipeg MB when MB is available in the configured region data.\par
- Date -- duration header lines such as 1991-09-21 -- 51:27 retain the date as header/date evidence.\par
- Generic venue-list fallback excludes comma-separated performer lists and no longer accepts one-word venue terms of five or fewer normalized characters merely as substrings of longer lines; standalone and explicit Venue: forms remain valid.\par


\par\b Build 396 - Setlist filename artist and title-like location protection\b0\par
- A single-word Artist DB hit embedded inside a longer selected-setlist filename now requires positional strong filename structure. A date qualifies only when it is the first substantive component after the artist, with ordinary filename separators allowed between them. Two Gentlemen.txt and Two Gentlemen 1979-10-05.txt cannot promote Artist Two merely because Two exists in artists.sqlite; Prince 1984-06-07.txt remains valid.\par
- Unlabeled trailing full-state-name parsing rejects title-like city fragments ending in connectives/prepositions. Two Gentlemen In New York no longer becomes Two Gentlemen In, NY, while Albany New York remains valid.\par
- Generic path/String2 location parsing refuses a lone connective/preposition as the one-word city, preventing Two Gentlemen In, NY from becoming Venue Two Gentlemen / City In on re-inventory.\par

\par\b Build 397 - Technical review remediation and incremental duplicate fix\b0\par
- Tightened one-word selected-setlist filename artist matching so a date qualifies only as the first substantive component after the artist.\par
- Bounded selected-setlist header reads to the same 1 MiB sample / 16 MiB rejection policy used for other untrusted text inputs.\par
- Added a 180-second full-FLAC validation timeout to duplicate cleanup; an unverifiable keeper blocks relocation of that cluster.\par
- Pinned update checks to the official TLO GitHub repository and added bounded chunked downloads with a 1 GiB hard ceiling.\par
- The source-bundle setlistFM.py utility now uses the same production setlist.fm lookup, quotas, rate-limit coordination, and TLOHome state as Inventory.\par
- macOS Trash operations now pass folder paths as data to osascript rather than embedding the path in AppleScript source.\par
- Fixed Add Shows duplicate detection so a matching date is insufficient by itself: the same existing bootlist Show cell must also contain independently validated artist evidence. Raw show names and folder basenames cannot directly satisfy the artist predicate.\par
- Artist duplicate matching uses normalized whole-token/whole-phrase boundaries, so short artists such as Yes do not match unrelated names such as Hayes; valid Artist DB masters and aliases remain accepted.\par
\par\b Build 398 - Corrupt-audio Trash/Recycle Bin policy\b0\par
- Corruption percentage is calculated from one pre-removal audio-file snapshot.\par
- An all-corrupt logical show is always sent to Trash/Recycle Bin regardless of acceptable corruption %, including when the setting is 100.\par
- When the logical show remains, an all-corrupt physical music folder is sent to Trash/Recycle Bin when it can be removed independently without removing healthy nested music folders.\par
- Every other detected corrupt audio file is sent individually to Trash/Recycle Bin before tag/copy/rename work continues.\par
- Successful folder/file removals are pruned from the active group and metadata paths so later stages cannot reuse stale paths.\par
- If a whole-folder or all-corrupt-folder Trash operation fails, TLO logs the failure and still attempts the corrupt files individually; no permanent-delete fallback is used.\par
\par
\par\b Build 399 - Cross-partition potential duplicate handling\b0\par
- When Add Shows Process New Shows runs with Check for Duplicates, matching bootlist rows are classified by storage partition before an unavailable comparison is requested.\par
- If every potential match is definitively on another partition, the incoming show remains accepted and is marked (pdupNN), beginning at (pdup01) and advancing beyond the highest existing pdup number in the matching family.\par
- Rooted drive/mount identity takes precedence; volume-label comparison is used for legacy rows without a rooted path. Unknown partition identity preserves the prior duplicate-review behavior rather than guessing.\par
- Same-partition matches continue through the existing duplicate/copy/alternate handling without change.\par
- The pdup qualifier propagates through Show/setlist/tag identity and through Rename Compliantly when enabled; without Rename Compliantly, the original physical folder name is preserved.\par
\par\b Build 400 - Corruption verification and fail-closed Trash safety\b0\par
- Audio that cannot be read is no longer automatically classified as corrupt. Permission, I/O, unavailable-volume, memory/resource, directory-listing, and unexpected validator errors make the corruption check unverifiable.\par
- If corruption verification is incomplete for a logical show, TLO does not Trash anything for corruption and skips rename, tag, Copy/Delete, and SHN-conversion mutations for that show. Inventory metadata may still be recorded.\par
- A malformed or unrecognized audio stream that the validator can actually read remains proven corrupt. The existing rule that an all-proven-corrupt folder is trashed regardless of acceptable corruption % remains in effect.\par
- On Windows, TLO now requests Recycle Bin deletion through IFileOperation with explicit recycle-only/fail-early behavior. If Windows cannot recycle the item, TLO reports the failure and leaves it in place; there is no permanent-delete fallback.\par
- Dry Run identifies unverifiable corruption checks and reports WOULD_NOT_TRASH instead of treating read errors as corruption.\par
\par\b Build 401 - Exact setlist families and literal path enumeration\b0\par
- TLO now treats only the exact setlist filename for a Show and its canonical (altN) collision variants as one setlist family. A longer Show name that merely starts with the same text is not included.\par
- Re-inventory cleanup therefore cannot delete a retained longer-named Show's setlist simply because its filename shares a prefix with a replaced Show. Incremental setlist review uses the same exact-family rule.\par
- TLOHome, logs, and setlists directory paths are opened literally rather than being embedded in glob patterns. Legal directory characters such as [ and ] no longer cause silent misses in postprocess, Research, Reverse Copy/Delete log discovery, or Add Shows setlist enumeration.\par
\par\b Build 402 - Verified Copy/Delete and reverse-restore safety\b0\par
- Cross-partition Copy/Delete now requires complete recursive SHA-256 tree identity, in addition to path/size/structure verification, before the original folder is deleted. A read, stat, or hash failure aborts deletion.\par
- Same-partition Copy/Delete remains a direct rename/move and does not perform unnecessary size or hash checks. Ordinary Tag Copy keeps size/structure verification because it does not delete the source.\par
- Copy operations use an operation-owned temporary sibling. A failed copy/verification is cleaned up without touching any pre-existing destination.\par
- Reverse Copy/Delete validates containment before creating directories, verifies cross-partition restores in a temporary sibling, and never deletes an original directory during rollback.\par
\par\b Build 403 - Safer duplicate repair and removal\b0\par
Duplicate FLAC validation now distinguishes a confirmed decoder failure from an unverifiable validation attempt. I/O, resource, timeout, or validator-infrastructure failures do not count as proof of corruption. If the kept folder has an unrepaired or unverifiable FLAC, TLO leaves its duplicate candidates in place for later review. Replacement audio is staged and byte-verified before installation. In Add Shows duplicate review, Delete New / Keep Old now sends the new folder to Trash/Recycle Bin and fails without deleting it if Trash/Recycle Bin is unavailable.\par
\par\b Build 404 - Safer update verification and scanner paths\b0\par
Update downloads now reject redirects outside the approved GitHub HTTPS hosts and require a valid SHA-256 digest before an update ZIP is accepted. A missing or mismatched digest causes the download to fail rather than being treated as verified. Windows custom malware-scanner commands still use the configured trusted scanner template, but TLO supplies the artifact path through an environment variable instead of inserting the raw path into cmd.exe text.\par
\par\b Build 405 - Cancellation log safety and CI GUI coverage\b0\par
When an active inventory run is cancelled, TLO deletes only tokenized logs whose tokens were newly allocated by that interrupted run. Reused tokens from an earlier inventory are preserved. Log cleanup validates tokens before constructing deletion paths, so malformed token values cannot escape the logs directory. This is a cancellation-safety change and does not alter normal completed-run logs. The source regression suite also attempts to provide a virtual X display on Linux CI so Tkinter behavior tests execute; if GUI/display tests still have to skip, CI prints a prominent coverage warning.\par
\par\b Build 406 - GitHub regression-fixture stabilization\b0\par
- Application runtime behavior is unchanged from Build 405; this build removes environment-dependent false failures on Linux hosts with a working gio trash backend.\par
- Five historical tests that exercise inventory orchestration rather than audio validation now explicitly treat their placeholder .flac bytes as healthy for corruption classification.\par
- The tests do not disable Trash/Recycle Bin itself; dedicated corruption tests continue to validate corruption and Trash behavior.\par

\par\b Build 407 - All-Star artist fallback and Research results search\b0\par
- Terminal All Star/All Stars variants now use the same secondary Artist DB fallback as terminal Band. Supported forms include spaces, dashes, no separator between All and Star, and singular/plural spellings. The complete artist candidate is still searched first, and only one unique shorter match is accepted.\par
- The Research input Search button stays the live default whenever its dialog is active; Return and keypad Enter invoke it from anywhere in the dialog.\par
- The read-only Research results window now supports Ctrl-A select-all, plus a Search button that is also invoked by Ctrl-F.\par
- The results find dialog contains Search for:, Forward and Backwards radio buttons with Forward as the default, repeated case-insensitive directional searching, and wraparound at either end.\par
\par\b Build 408 - Generic song-title placeholders\b0\par
When a song title from a setlist or from an existing audio Title tag is exactly Title, Titled, or Titles after normal cleanup, TLO writes Unknown for that track. The comparison is case-insensitive. A numbered setlist row still keeps its track position, so later songs do not shift. Numbered title tags such as 01. Title are cleaned before the check and also become Unknown. Longer legitimate names such as The Title Track, A Song Titled Sue, and Titles Are Hard are not changed.
\par\b Build 409 - Numbered setlist safety and blank tracks\b0\par
A leading bare track number such as 01 is retained as an Unknown-title track when the next meaningful numbered song row confirms the sequence, such as 02 Day Waves. Short numeric-only rows no longer start an unstructured unnumbered-title block, while numeric song names such as 69 and 1999 remain valid when embedded inside an established song block. Compact parenthetical personnel credits such as Gary Burton(vib) and Chick Corea (p), and duration summaries such as min 38:24, are excluded from unnumbered title fallback. When at least three consecutive numbered song rows provide convincing numbered-setlist evidence, TLO does not assemble a replacement setlist from later unnumbered prose merely because its count happens to match the audio files. The reported Chick Corea & Gary Burton example therefore resolves to Unknown, Day Waves, Desert Air, Crystal Silence, Children Song No. 1, and La Fiesta.

\par\b Build 410 - Explicit setlists and multi-disc track ordering\b0\par
TLO now recognizes punctuation-separated collector filenames such as CJM-Disc01,Track01.flac as true disc/track pairs and orders all disc-1 tracks before disc-2 tracks. In an explicit unnumbered Setlist:, Songs:, or Tracks: block, Notes: and similar terminators stop track parsing even when no numbered songs were present, so later note paragraphs beginning 1), 2), 3) cannot replace the real song list. Legitimate titles such as Thank The Nurse That's Nursing You remain valid, while explicit thank-you prose such as Thanks to the taper remains a terminator. Enc: is accepted as an encore prefix and is removed before the Title tag is written. The reported Country Joe 2001-05-26 example now uses all 19 titles from the Setlist block, ending with This Land Is Your Land.
\par\b Build 411 - Track-list candidate selection and corroboration\b0\par
Before padding a short numbered setlist candidate with Unknown titles, TLO now checks for a plausible local unnumbered song list whose count exactly matches the audio files. Audio-count agreement is primary evidence, so three numbered collector notes cannot displace a 17-title song block when there are 17 audio tracks. When numbered and unnumbered candidates both have the exact audio count, matching audio filenames and existing Title tags can positively reinforce either candidate. Missing, generic, unreadable, or unrelated filename/tag values are neutral and never count against a candidate. A short numbered block embedded in surrounding prose or collector notes may lose an otherwise unsupported equal-count tie to a contiguous unnumbered song block; outside that structural case, normal numbered-list precedence remains. The reported Cramps 1984-06-25 example now uses all 17 titles from Devil With A Blue Dress On through She Said rather than the numbered processing notes 1) Boost the volume, 2) Adjust the speed, and 3) Retrack the entire show.

\par\b Build 412 - Explicit Set/CD/Disc/Disk track regions\b0\par
When an unnumbered setlist contains an explicit Set, Disc, Disk, CD, Setlist, Track List, Songs, or Tracks boundary, TLO now treats that heading as the start of the song region and discards plausible short header text above it from title candidates. Later Set/Disc/Disk/CD headings and Encore continue the ordered region rather than resetting earlier titles. Blank lines may separate song blocks. Date-labelled filler/continuation dividers such as on July 7, 1967: are not titles but do not end the region; Notes, source/checksum/technical markers, and decorative separator lines do end it. In the reported Reverend Gary Davis 1967-07-08 example, the 16 audio files now receive the 16 real titles from Introduction through Samson and Delilah. Reverend Gary Davis, Friends' Center, Seattle, WA, and SBD > ? > CDr > EAC > FLAC above Set 1 are no longer used to fill the title count.

\par\b Build 413 - Dated subsection boundaries and numbering continuity\b0\par
Valid date-prefixed broadcast/show subsection headings with trailing venue, source, or broadcast context are now treated as boundaries rather than numbered tracks. A line such as 30.10.1948 - Royal Roost, NY - WMCA Radio broadcast can no longer become track 30 or be normalized into the next audio position. When a numbered list has already established the expected next number, a provisional higher-number row is discarded if the next numbered row resumes that exact expected value. Confirmed large numbering gaps remain supported when subsequent numbering reinforces the jump, such as 01, 02, 30, 31. TLO also repairs the narrow lost-newline form where an apostrophe-style duration is immediately followed by a valid dated subsection heading, keeping the song row while removing the attached heading text. In the reported Tadd Dameron Royal Roost 1948 example, the dated sections 16.10.1948, 23.10.1948, 30.10.1948, and 06.11.1948 remain non-title boundaries and the fourteen songs stay in track order 01 through 14.

\par\b Build 414 - Location-safe artist headers and bare-number tracks\b0\par
Generic path artist scanning no longer promotes a DB artist phrase when that occurrence is only the trailing city of a longer City, State/Region/Country path suffix. Thus Washington in My Recording 2015-04-10 Kennedy Center Washington, DC is treated as location text, while an exact artist directory named Washington remains eligible. Strict unlabeled setlist headers may contain an extra room/hall line between Artist and the named Venue; Artist / Concert Hall / Kennedy Center / Washington, DC / Date keeps the first line as Artist and Kennedy Center as Venue. A collaboration header such as Chick Corea and Herbie Hancock can be used when every component resolves uniquely in the Artist DB even if the combined phrase is not its own DB row; unresolved or ambiguous components are not guessed. When a contiguous bare-number block such as 1 through 11 exactly matches the audio-file count, TLO treats those values as track positions and writes Unknown for each Title rather than writing the numbers as song names. The reported Chick Corea and Herbie Hancock 2015-04-10 Kennedy Center case therefore resolves the collaboration artist correctly and assigns eleven Unknown song titles.

\par Build 415 - Copy/Delete destination replacement scope0\par
Tag Copy/Delete Original now treats its selected destination parent as an append container rather than a subtree re-inventory scope. Copying/deleting a small source tree into an already inventoried destination such as /mnt/d/boots preserves unrelated existing bootlist rows and setlists beneath /boots. Postprocess may replace only an existing row whose normalized destination Volume and full Path exactly match a destination show path produced by the current run; child paths, sibling shows, and the rest of the destination tree remain untouched. Ordinary direct re-inventory of an explicitly requested existing tree is unchanged. The reported case of 29 transferred records no longer selects 2,135 unrelated /boots rows for replacement.\par
\par\b Build 416 - Structured Live-at header and artist precedence\b0\par
Selected-setlist metadata now recognizes Artist followed by Live at Venue, City, State/Region/Country, Date as a high-confidence structured header before looser unlabeled Artist/Venue/Location shapes are tried. This prevents short introductory commentary from being promoted to Artist and prevents the real artist line from being shifted into Venue. A trailing performance date on the Live at line is removed before Venue and Location are split. Obvious duplicated terminal Group Group or Band Band copy noise is collapsed before Artist DB lookup; a single Group or Band suffix is left intact. When that structured artist resolves uniquely in artists.sqlite, it may replace a lower-confidence generic path-pattern or subdirectory artist under the existing precedence rule, but it does not override stronger sources such as $slam or DB-backed audio tags. The reported AL DiMeola Group GROUP / Live at G\u252?rzenich, Cologne, Germany, 1978-10-13 setlist therefore resolves to Artist Al DiMeola Group, Venue G\u252?rzenich, and Location Cologne, Germany.\par
\par\b Build 417 - Terminal Artist DB suffix fallback expansion\b0\par
The secondary Artist DB retry used for terminal Band and All Star/All Stars artist text now also recognizes terminal Group, All Star Band, All-Star Band, All Stars Band, and All-Stars Band. The complete potential artist name is always queried first and wins when present. Only after a complete-name miss does TLO remove one recognized terminal suffix and retry; the shorter result must resolve uniquely, and nonterminal occurrences are never stripped.\par
\par\b Build 418 - Restored terminal suffix performance artists\b0\par
When the guarded Band/Group/All-Star-family fallback identifies a unique base artist, TLO now restores the exact terminal suffix text that was removed for the lookup and uses the expanded result as the performance artist. The full candidate is still queried first, ambiguous base matches remain unresolved, and a DB master that already ends in a supported grouping suffix is not given a duplicate suffix. Restored expanded performance artists that are not themselves full Artist DB masters are recorded in TLOHome/artistsNotInDatabase.txt; that persistent review list preserves previous entries and merges, sorts, and de-duplicates new entries case-insensitively.\par
